<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
            http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!--
      Two look edits, both through tokens (DESIGN §2.1): components never hold colours.

      1) accent-clean: "the monster was not drunk" in the weekend scene (DESIGN §4.2).
         It used accent, and on wave 01 that is #e2604c, almost the same tone as danger
         #d2553f. So "drank" and "did not drink" differed by one word, while the colour in both
         cases said "alarm". A green is needed.
         Why NOT accent-code: it is named by the role "another activity channel" (git), and
         borrowing it here would repeat the mistake its own comment in 0270
         warns about. Why NOT success: it holds the links of the discipline
         trail, and "did not drink" would sound like one more item passed, while the monster at
         weekends lives apart from discipline; that is the whole point of the scene.
         The wave picks the shade itself: 01 a mossy one (next to the peach it reads as
         "good"), 02 its cold arcade green.

      2) cal-weekend / cal-othermonth: weekends in the calendar grid (DESIGN §5).
         A weekend was LIGHTER than a future day (bg-surface-muted), so in the future
         half of the window the Sat/Sun columns looked lighter than weekdays and did not read at all:
         the difference stayed visible only in the header, where the labels lie on the tile background.
         The weekend is lowered below a future day, and the neighbouring month below the weekend, so that
         the lightness ladder stays unambiguous:
           an ordinary day > a future weekday > a weekend > the neighbouring month.

      An UPDATE over the applied 0080-theme-seed (the seed's checksum must not be touched); `||`
      overwrites the existing keys and adds a new one. The same values sit as
      a CSS fallback in wave-01.css: the board does not go white if the DB is unavailable.
    -->
    <changeSet id="0290-wave-01-clean-and-weekend" author="danchuo">
        <sql><![CDATA[
UPDATE theme SET tokens = tokens || $J${
  "accent-clean": "#5f9e52",
  "cal-weekend": "#f0e1d8",
  "cal-othermonth": "#e3d1c4"
}$J$::jsonb WHERE key = 'wave-01';
        ]]></sql>
        <rollback><![CDATA[
UPDATE theme SET tokens = (tokens - 'accent-clean') || $J${
  "cal-weekend": "#f7ece8",
  "cal-othermonth": "#ece0d7"
}$J$::jsonb WHERE key = 'wave-01';
        ]]></rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <!--
      Wave 02 does not render the weekend scene (the hasWeekendScene capability is only 01's for now),
      but it still needs the token: without it it would inherit wave 01's mossy green
      from :root, which is foreign to Obscura's cold palette. 02's calendar is blue: the same
      ladder edit there, only in its tones.
    -->
    <changeSet id="0290-wave-02-clean-and-weekend" author="danchuo">
        <sql><![CDATA[
UPDATE theme SET tokens = tokens || $J${
  "accent-clean": "#2e9e6e",
  "cal-weekend": "#c8e0f7",
  "cal-othermonth": "#b6d3ee"
}$J$::jsonb WHERE key = 'wave-02';
        ]]></sql>
        <rollback><![CDATA[
UPDATE theme SET tokens = (tokens - 'accent-clean') || $J${
  "cal-weekend": "#d8ebfd",
  "cal-othermonth": "#cbe1f6"
}$J$::jsonb WHERE key = 'wave-02';
        ]]></rollback>
    </changeSet>
</databaseChangeLog>
